A recurrence relation is a mathematical equation that describes a sequence of values, each of which is a function of the preceding values. In the context of algorithmic scrutiny, recurrence relations are used to describe the time complexity of algorithms, particularly those that use recursion.

The time complexity of an algorithm is a measure of how the running time of the algorithm grows as the size of the input increases. For recursive algorithms, the running time can often be described using a recurrence relation. This relation expresses the running time of the algorithm in terms of the running times of smaller instances of the same algorithm.

For example, consider the recursive algorithm for computing the factorial of a number:

```
function factorial(n) {
    if (n == 0) {
        return 1;
    } else {
        return n * factorial(n - 1);
    }
}
```

The time complexity of this algorithm can be described by the recurrence relation T(n) = T(n - 1) + O(1), where T(n) is the time complexity of the algorithm for an input of size n, and O(1) is the constant time it takes to perform the multiplication and the comparison.

Solving recurrence relations is an important part of algorithmic scrutiny, as it allows us to understand how the time complexity of an algorithm grows as the size of the input increases. This is critical for determining the algorithm's efficiency and for comparing it to other algorithms.

