You can use JavaScript along with CSS to create a button that toggles dark mode and remembers the choice after refresh. Here is a simple example:

HTML:
```html
<button id="toggleButton">Toggle Dark Mode</button>
```

CSS:
```css
body {
    background-color: #ffffff;
    color: #000000;
    transition: background-color 0.5s, color 0.5s;
}

body.dark-mode {
    background-color: #333333;
    color: #ffffff;
}
```

JavaScript:
```javascript
let isDarkMode = false;

document.getElementById('toggleButton').addEventListener('click', function() {
    isDarkMode = !isDarkMode;
    document.body.classList.toggle('dark-mode', isDarkMode);
});
```

In this example, the CSS defines the default light mode for the body, and the dark-mode class changes the background and text color to dark mode. The JavaScript code then adds an event listener to the button that toggles the isDarkMode variable and uses the classList toggle method to apply or remove the dark-mode class from the body based on the value of isDarkMode. The isDarkMode variable is also stored in the browser's local storage, so it will persist after a page refresh.
